import "json/importer.ct"
import json("data.json")


module(UsesJson)
  # # Using the JSON importer
    The same two lines @usesPython.ct@ writes, over a file of data rather than of code. The first
    brings in a page which declares an importer; the second uses that importer by the name it
    declared. Nothing in the compiler knows what `json` means at the point the second line is read —
    it is looked for among the importers declared by the pages imported above it, which is exactly
    one page, which declares exactly one.
    What arrives is a page holding one declaration, whose body is @data.json@ as a [/Data/Json]. The
    name it is declared under is built from the path, so it is not one written here: this page is
    the case that the import resolves and the value is built, and reaching the value by name is what
    the integration fixture at the top level does.
    It lives under @artifacts/@ so the integration suite does not compile it, as @usesPython.ct@
    does and for its reason: the page reaches @hsrawprgm()@, which is worth compiling once rather
    than on every run.
